  "answer_text": "The safe temperature at which food can be accepted during delivery depends on the type of food. These standard guidelines are based on the **FDA Food Code** and **ServSafe** standards:\n\n### 1. Cold TCS Foods (Time/Temperature Control for Safety)\nMost refrigerated foods\u2014including fresh meat, poultry, fish, dairy, and pre-cut produce\u2014must be received at **41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower**.\n\n### 2. Hot TCS Foods\nAny hot, pre-cooked foods being delivered must be received at **135\u00b0F (57\u00b0C) or higher**.\n\n### 3. Frozen Foods\nFrozen foods must be received **frozen solid**. \n* **When to reject:** Reject the delivery if you see large ice crystals on the food or packaging, water stains, or fluids at the bottom of the case. These are signs of \"time-temperature abuse,\" meaning the food thawed and was refrozen.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Special Exceptions (The \"45\u00b0F Rule\")\nCertain foods can be accepted at slightly higher temperatures but **must be cooled to 41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower within 4 hours** of delivery:\n\n* **Milk:** Can be received at **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C)** or lower.\n* **Shell Eggs:** Can be received at an ambient (air) temperature of **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C)** or lower.\n* **Shucked Shellfish:** Can be received at **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C)** or lower.\n* **Live Shellfish (oysters, mussels, clams, scallops):** Can be received at an ambient (air) temperature of **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C)**, with an internal temperature no higher than **50\u00b0F (10\u00b0C)**.\n\n---\n\n### How to Correctly Measure Temperatures on Delivery:\n* **Meat, Poultry, and Fish:** Insert the thermometer probe directly into the thickest part of the food (usually the center).\n* **ROP (Reduced Oxygen Packaging / Vacuum-Packed / Sous Vide):** Insert the thermometer probe between two packages. **Do not puncture** the packaging.\n* **Other Packaged Liquids/Tubs (like milk cartons or sour cream):** Open the container and insert the thermometer probe, making sure the probe is fully submerged and does not touch the sides or bottom of the container.",
